<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch10_P02_Counterexamples_to_the_claim</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here again is the proof that \(\sqrt{9}\) is irrational.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Assume that \(\sqrt{9} = \dfrac{a}{b}\), where \(a\) and \(b\) are integers with \(b \neq 0\), and \(a\) and \(b\) are coprime (they have no common factor other than 1).</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Then \(9 = \dfrac{a^2}{b^2}\),</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>so \(9b^2 = a^2\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Therefore \(a^2\) is a multiple of 9,</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>so \(a\) is a multiple of 9, and we can write \(a = 9k\) for some integer \(k\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Then \(9b^2 = (9k)^2 = 81k^2\),</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>so \(b^2 = 9k^2\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Therefore \(b^2\) is a multiple of 9,</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>so \(b\) is a multiple of 9.</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>So \(a\) and \(b\) are both multiples of 9, and they cannot be coprime.</td></tr>
<tr><td>11</td><td>This is a contradiction, so \(\sqrt{9}\) is irrational.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Line 5 uses this claim: if \(a^2\) is a multiple of 9, then \(a\) is a multiple of 9.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Give an example of an integer \(a\) for which \(a^2\) is a multiple of 9 but \(a\) is not a multiple of 9.</p>
<div>\(a =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>The proof uses the same false claim once more. In which line?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
<li><p>The claim is false for other numbers too. Give an example of an integer \(a\) for which \(a^2\) is a multiple of {@nn@} but \(a\) is not a multiple of {@nn@}.</p>
<div>\(a =\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>To test a general claim, try some values: one counterexample shows that the claim is false, and so that a step relying on it does not follow.</p>
<p>(a) The claim fails for every multiple of 3 that is not a multiple of 9. For example, \(a = 3\) gives \(a^2 = 9\), and \(a = 6\) gives \(a^2 = 36 = 9 \times 4\), but neither 3 nor 6 is a multiple of 9. What is true is weaker: if \(a^2\) is a multiple of 9, then \(a\) is a multiple of 3, because 3 is prime.</p>
<p>(b) Line 9 makes the same false step for \(b\): it concludes that \(b\) is a multiple of 9 because \(b^2\) is.</p>
<p>(c) For {@nn@}, \(a = {@cexn@}\) works: \({@cexn^2@} = {@nn@} \times {@cexn^2/nn@}\), but {@cexn@} is not a multiple of {@nn@}. The claim <q>if \(a^2\) is a multiple of \(N\), then \(a\) is a multiple of \(N\)</q> is true when \(N\) is prime, which is why the same method proves that \(\sqrt{2}\) and \(\sqrt{3}\) are irrational (see the chapter on proof by contradiction). It fails whenever \(N\) has a square factor greater than 1, as {@nn@} does.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
